**The dao.jing.content decision: sound and minimal.** The four roles map one-to-one onto the existing decomposition (handlers/`serve-content!`→`serve-step`; `remote.step`→`dao.jing.content.step`; `connect-content!`→driver; `remote.async`→async), and the coordinate-as-two-remote-descriptors is the right p2p shape: it names two streams instead of a server endpoint, works over any channel type, and stays serializable. Consumers all accounted: coordinate (`dao.space.query.cljc:250`), `yin.repl.link` (`link.cljc:184`), linker M3/M4, btree async, tests (slice 4 proof: "no require of `dao.jing.remote` remains"). The "three copies become one" claim is grounded — `remote.cljc:35`, `step.cljc:258-259`, `linker.cljc:1219,1309` — with the honest strengthening that only copy 1 includes the canonical CBOR decode. Two imprecisions: the driver's consumer list says `dao.space.index` and `yin.vm.content`, but the tree's coordinate opener is `dao.space.query:250` and `yin.vm/content.cljc` has no remote path at all (its handle is an argument); and §8 spells four namespaces (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`, `.step`, `.driver`, `.async`) while §9 slice 4 says "`dao.jing.content` with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver`, `async`". SHOULD 6, 12.

**Named functions**: every existing name verified above; every new name (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`, `serve-step`, `dao.jing.content.step`, `driver`, `async`, `mirror-step`, `middleware/apply-request|wrap|gate|present`, `dao.stream.udp`, `.pair`, `.meet`) is stated as new with a creating slice. ✓

